{"action_id":null,"arguments":[],"assumptions":[{"assumption_id":"node.4053a6065a703c4e1349ce6a.a0","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"The selected slice identifies unit 0 as paragraph 10 and units 3, 5 and 6 as paragraphs 14, 15 and 16.","status":"PROVISIONAL"},{"assumption_id":"node.4053a6065a703c4e1349ce6a.a1","provenance_refs":[],"statement":"Continuing responsibility can coexist with delegation of operational tasks.","status":"PROVISIONAL"}],"bundle_hash":null,"candidate_id":"candidate.11606afe93494b6d8b35a5797d9877a8","controlled_language":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 can be read principally as retaining full and ultimate accountability with the Product Provider despite outsourcing. Governance and contractual allocation, backed by actual authority and conduct consistent with that allocation, could support a conclusion that responsibility was retained. They do not alone establish sound operations or proper ownership records. Section 6.8 permits specified actors to maintain the register and separately requires requested disclosure of its location; it does not displace the provider's responsibility. Section 6.11C expressly prescribes care in selection and monitoring for valuation outsourcing, but the packet does not make that standard the tokenisation fulfillment test.","disposition_reason":"Unreviewed interpretation proposal","family_ids":["scope"],"generation_phase":"BLIND_INITIAL","issue_ids":[],"parent_id":null,"probability_of_legal_correctness":null,"revision_reason":"This reading treats paragraph 10 primarily as allocating non-transferable accountability. A service failure need not prove that responsibility was transferred, although it may prove a separate performance failure. It does not treat contractual allocation alone as fulfillment of all operational requirements.","run_id":"run.8adc12b432874c5bb1e8b4730a0ab93f","scheduling_priority":0,"schema_version":"interpretation.v1","score_purpose":"INVESTIGATION_PRIORITY_ONLY","source_packet_hash":"d74180e126dda